  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nWinds generally out of the west have picked up a little today,\nas temperatures quickly surged into the mid-90s, with highs\nprojected to be in the upper 90s to near 100, challenging more\nrecord highs. However, convective initiation has begun in Perry\nCounty, MO and just north of Wabash County, IL. Storm\ndevelopment has begun sooner than most every model, with the\nmore active FV3 the closest. Isolated to scattered thunderstorms\nare forecast to continue this afternoon through the evening,\nmainly in the northern half of the Quad State where a frontal\nboundary is positioned. Robust CAPE and PWs near the 95th\npercentile are present, but mid-level lapse rates are fairly low\nand shear is very limited. A few, probably sub-severe, decent\nwind gusts are possible with the strongest storms. \n\nWinds calm overnight with storm activity waning. The boundary\nlifts northward tomorrow, reducing storm chances for the region\nthis weekend. However, slight chance PoPs were introduced in the\nfar northeast Saturday and far north Sunday during the afternoon\nhours to line up with the D2 and D3 marginal risk areas. Most\nlikely, storm activity will be outside the CWA, but a slight\nshift southward could bring a storm or two to the area.\nConvective environment will generally be similar to today, with\nplenty of CAPE and moisture, but very limited shear. Highs again\nwill challenge heat records, especially if dew points trend a\nlittle lower through the weekend.\n\nA building high pressure system over Ontario helps push the\nfrontal boundary southward through the Quad State as a cold\nfront Sunday night into Tuesday. Showers and thunderstorms are\npossible Monday to Monday night, with a few stronger storms\npossible. Southeastern portions of the Quad State might still\nreach a triple digit heat index Monday before colder air moves\nin.\n\nAfterwards, an upper low located in NorCal/Oregon stalls out\nover the Midwest, helping to keep northerly flow across the Quad\nState and bringing temperatures down to near normal with highs\nin the upper 70s to near 80 and lows in the upper 50s to near\n60. There is around a 40-70% chance (higher in the north, lower\nin the south) of highs below 80 each day from Tuesday through\nFriday.",
